1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,120
And now for you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:08,060 --> 00:00:16,280
I have a book that was published in 1952 by a Scoutmaster by the name of Rice E. Cochran.

3
00:00:16,540 --> 00:00:17,480
Here's something interesting.

4
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:22,960
I rapidly acquired a reputation for supernatural wisdom by saying nothing.

5
00:00:23,560 --> 00:00:26,000
As the patrols pitched camp and cooked their meals,

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,660
I simply strolled about watching everything with an Olympian air,

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,500
and when a patrol had difficulty getting its fire started,

8
00:00:33,620 --> 00:00:38,160
I merely stood and watched silently to the deep embarrassment of the patrol.

9
00:00:39,100 --> 00:00:43,780
He's not saying nothing, but he's thinking plenty, I heard one boy mutter to another.

10
00:00:44,180 --> 00:00:45,500
Okay, so which one are you?

11
00:00:46,019 --> 00:00:48,000
Are you not saying nothing but thinking plenty?

12
00:00:48,820 --> 00:00:50,500
Or saying plenty but thinking nothing?

13
00:00:51,720 --> 00:00:54,500
This is podcast number seven.

14
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:00,560
Hey!

15
00:01:00,660 --> 00:01:00,780
Hey!

16
00:01:02,720 --> 00:01:03,580
Hey!

17
00:01:03,580 --> 00:01:26,840
Hi, welcome back to the Scoutmasters podcast.

18
00:01:27,120 --> 00:01:30,380
This is Clark Green in Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

19
00:01:30,380 --> 00:01:34,460
We're going to have a little discussion about being a Scoutmaster or being a legislator.

20
00:01:35,160 --> 00:01:41,140
This involves troop policy manuals and job descriptions and contracts and things of that ilk.

21
00:01:41,740 --> 00:01:46,240
Then I have a story about a buddy of mine, George, who used to work for me at Scout Camp,

22
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:49,300
and a little something that George and I did while we were at camp.

23
00:01:49,360 --> 00:01:53,060
And I have to tell you parenthetically that George worked for me years ago,

24
00:01:53,060 --> 00:01:58,600
and now he has a son who is of an age to join our troop,

25
00:01:58,600 --> 00:02:01,839
which is going to be happening within the next week or so,

26
00:02:02,339 --> 00:02:07,399
which is making me feel a little old, but it's a happy moment.

27
00:02:07,740 --> 00:02:14,160
Our second installment of our series of moving a troop from being adult-led to youth-led,

28
00:02:14,900 --> 00:02:18,260
and then a Scoutmasters minute about giving.

29
00:02:19,459 --> 00:02:24,620
And that will wind up the podcast, so let's get ourselves started, shall we?

30
00:02:24,920 --> 00:02:25,980
Why, of course we shall.

31
00:02:25,980 --> 00:02:30,420
Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

32
00:02:38,079 --> 00:02:41,040
You know, as a new Scoutmaster, I noticed a couple things about Scouts,

33
00:02:41,399 --> 00:02:46,519
and I think it's something that most newer Scoutmasters recognize.

34
00:02:46,519 --> 00:02:48,500
They're not very serious about stuff sometimes.

35
00:02:49,320 --> 00:02:50,959
No, they don't take things seriously.

36
00:02:50,960 --> 00:02:54,300
They don't seem to have a real good grasp on responsibility.

37
00:02:54,640 --> 00:02:56,240
It can be downright aggravating.

38
00:02:57,340 --> 00:03:02,940
Along with all the great stuff, all the great energy and the desire for discovery,

39
00:03:03,240 --> 00:03:06,440
and as nice and as kind as they can be, sometimes they're just aggravating.

40
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:09,200
Well, I don't know if this is news to you,

41
00:03:09,260 --> 00:03:12,300
but that's basically what happens when you're in your teenage years.

42
00:03:12,520 --> 00:03:14,460
That's basically what happens when you're an adolescent.

43
00:03:14,460 --> 00:03:22,380
So I came up with a strategy that was not unusual to me to try and solve what I saw as problems,

44
00:03:22,920 --> 00:03:28,000
and that was to go ahead and become a legislator and write a manual for our troop

45
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:31,880
about chiefly concentrating on three things.

46
00:03:32,020 --> 00:03:35,580
One was attendance, which was very provoking,

47
00:03:35,580 --> 00:03:42,100
and two was discipline, which wasn't as big a problem, to be very honest with you,

48
00:03:42,160 --> 00:03:44,520
but, you know, you've got to be ready, right?

49
00:03:44,600 --> 00:03:45,120
Be prepared.

50
00:03:45,840 --> 00:03:48,620
And the third one was leadership.

51
00:03:49,460 --> 00:03:56,080
So this manual was, of course, a work of great area addition and wisdom,

52
00:03:57,040 --> 00:04:02,260
and we got it photocopied and showed it to the boys,

53
00:04:02,260 --> 00:04:04,420
and they were not impressed.

54
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:07,179
They were not impressed at all.

55
00:04:07,700 --> 00:04:13,060
I doubt whether one of them ever actually read it.

56
00:04:14,760 --> 00:04:21,279
What it was chiefly useful for was to quote to miscreants,

57
00:04:21,700 --> 00:04:23,060
you know, you're late for the meeting,

58
00:04:23,320 --> 00:04:28,099
and it says right in the manual that we gave you that you're supposed to be on time,

59
00:04:28,100 --> 00:04:34,180
or you missed this campout, and, well, we know that you're supposed to go on campouts.

60
00:04:34,580 --> 00:04:39,900
Apparently, that was not the magic switch that was going to turn them into responsible adults.

61
00:04:40,220 --> 00:04:41,540
It didn't happen.

62
00:04:42,340 --> 00:04:44,900
We got so frustrated, I remember this very clearly,

63
00:04:45,260 --> 00:04:48,000
me and a couple of my assistant scoutmasters,

64
00:04:48,100 --> 00:04:52,080
we got so frustrated with patrol leaders and things like that,

65
00:04:52,180 --> 00:04:54,680
kind of, you know, not being mature adults

66
00:04:54,680 --> 00:04:57,040
and taking their responsibilities seriously,

67
00:04:57,040 --> 00:05:01,340
that we decided we would trot out a set of job descriptions and contracts

68
00:05:01,340 --> 00:05:04,960
and ask them to sign a contract

69
00:05:04,960 --> 00:05:08,140
so that they would truly be committed to their leadership position.

70
00:05:08,260 --> 00:05:10,720
They would understand the weight and importance of it, as we did.

71
00:05:10,720 --> 00:05:13,760
The contract was as useless as the manual

72
00:05:13,760 --> 00:05:19,240
because the contract did not automatically turn my teenage patrol leaders

73
00:05:19,240 --> 00:05:22,500
into wise, considerate, skilled leaders.

74
00:05:22,500 --> 00:05:28,280
What does that is a lot of mentoring and coaching and a decent attitude

75
00:05:28,280 --> 00:05:33,960
and the realization that these areas of discipline and attendance and leadership

76
00:05:33,960 --> 00:05:37,000
are not problems that you're supposed to solve.

77
00:05:37,720 --> 00:05:40,240
They are the price of doing business when you're doing business

78
00:05:40,240 --> 00:05:42,720
with people who are going through their adolescence.

79
00:05:43,280 --> 00:05:44,580
That's the way that it works.

80
00:05:45,500 --> 00:05:47,900
And you need to understand a few things about adolescence,

81
00:05:47,980 --> 00:05:49,900
and you need to understand a few things about scouting,

82
00:05:50,040 --> 00:05:51,540
and then things get a little bit smoother.

83
00:05:51,540 --> 00:05:55,900
And I think one of the big ones for me was understanding that

84
00:05:55,900 --> 00:05:57,820
I was not supposed to be fixing problems.

85
00:05:57,940 --> 00:06:02,260
I was supposed to be growing decent human beings and decent leaders.

86
00:06:02,940 --> 00:06:04,920
And that if I concentrated my efforts on that

87
00:06:04,920 --> 00:06:08,240
instead of my creative writing exercises,

88
00:06:08,740 --> 00:06:12,480
that I would have much greater returns.

89
00:06:12,940 --> 00:06:15,540
Now, I've done a little informal survey online

90
00:06:15,540 --> 00:06:19,900
of troop handbooks and guidebooks and policy manuals

91
00:06:19,900 --> 00:06:23,420
and bylaws and things like that.

92
00:06:23,420 --> 00:06:25,880
And I have got to be honest with you.

93
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:30,080
I see that there is some utility in some of them,

94
00:06:30,220 --> 00:06:32,840
especially when it comes to financial matters and things like that.

95
00:06:33,280 --> 00:06:37,280
What I would like to see is I would like to see a troop policy manual

96
00:06:37,280 --> 00:06:40,940
on one 8.5 by 11 sheet of paper.

97
00:06:41,140 --> 00:06:46,359
I'm a big advocate of the 8.5 by 11 sheet of paper on its own, on one side.

98
00:06:46,760 --> 00:06:48,119
Now you're going to ask me,

99
00:06:48,200 --> 00:06:50,119
what happened to the policy manual that I wrote?

100
00:06:50,179 --> 00:06:51,059
Well, it got a lot smaller,

101
00:06:51,340 --> 00:06:53,280
and it became a troop family guide.

102
00:06:53,280 --> 00:07:00,640
And it is only just to kind of be like a frequently asked questions type thing

103
00:07:00,640 --> 00:07:03,340
about scouting for people who are unfamiliar with it.

104
00:07:04,520 --> 00:07:09,059
And mostly refers them to the salient parts of the scout handbook

105
00:07:09,059 --> 00:07:12,000
rather than trying to restate it in the manual.

106
00:07:12,320 --> 00:07:16,059
It also talks about some very basic logistic policies

107
00:07:16,059 --> 00:07:21,559
that we have as far as camping is concerned in gear and things like that.

108
00:07:21,560 --> 00:07:25,080
It does not read like the sea jackets manual

109
00:07:25,080 --> 00:07:27,960
or the uniform code of military justice.

110
00:07:28,460 --> 00:07:29,640
I can guarantee that.

111
00:07:30,240 --> 00:07:32,020
Even though that stuff is fun to write,

112
00:07:32,160 --> 00:07:33,800
it's the wrong tone for a scout troop.

113
00:07:35,380 --> 00:07:38,220
It is not full of negative reinforcements.

114
00:07:38,840 --> 00:07:40,980
What we try to do is reflect what scouting is,

115
00:07:41,080 --> 00:07:42,460
which is positive reinforcement,

116
00:07:43,240 --> 00:07:45,000
which is thinking about good things.

117
00:07:45,120 --> 00:07:47,760
It's not a system of demerits and difficulties.

118
00:07:47,760 --> 00:07:50,620
It's a chance for us to encourage.

119
00:07:51,460 --> 00:07:54,580
I would really closely examine any document that your troop might have

120
00:07:54,580 --> 00:07:56,440
and consider doing a bit of editing.

121
00:07:56,700 --> 00:07:57,740
Get out a big pair of shears.

122
00:07:57,920 --> 00:07:58,980
Garden shears would be good.

123
00:08:00,320 --> 00:08:01,159
Chainsaw, whatever.

124
00:08:02,520 --> 00:08:05,300
And see if you can get it on one 8.5 by 11 sheet of paper.

125
00:08:05,780 --> 00:08:06,219
I know.

126
00:08:06,800 --> 00:08:07,880
You're going to ask me the question,

127
00:08:08,039 --> 00:08:10,159
is mine on one 8.5 by 11 sheet of paper?

128
00:08:10,260 --> 00:08:11,020
No, it's not.

129
00:08:11,120 --> 00:08:12,300
Because, frankly,

130
00:08:12,539 --> 00:08:16,420
I've got better things to do right now than to try and reduce it down.

131
00:08:16,420 --> 00:08:22,020
I think we're on three sheets of 8.5 by 11 paper in booklet form.

132
00:08:22,940 --> 00:08:24,000
So maybe two.

133
00:08:24,740 --> 00:08:26,460
So I'm working on it.

134
00:08:26,760 --> 00:08:28,880
I have not attained perfection yet.

135
00:08:28,980 --> 00:08:31,760
And when I do, it will be announced on this podcast.

136
00:08:32,080 --> 00:08:32,700
That's for sure.

137
00:08:33,260 --> 00:08:39,000
So just a couple ideas about troop manuals, policy manuals, and the like.

138
00:08:39,000 --> 00:09:08,980
This has to be the truth, folks.

139
00:09:08,980 --> 00:09:15,360
Because there is no way anyone could make this up.

140
00:09:15,360 --> 00:09:24,100
So one of the things we're fiercely proud of at our scout camp is our retreat ceremony.

141
00:09:24,640 --> 00:09:28,460
It's styled after the West Point retreat ceremony that they have every evening.

142
00:09:29,260 --> 00:09:32,740
And we've had it going on for about 90 years.

143
00:09:32,880 --> 00:09:35,760
It's about 10 or 15 minutes worth of pomp and circumstance.

144
00:09:35,760 --> 00:09:42,660
And there's an actual band and a bugler and flags and marching and all kinds of great stuff.

145
00:09:43,560 --> 00:09:46,100
And years ago, I was on staff.

146
00:09:46,500 --> 00:09:51,740
And there was a special evening where we were going to have a retreat ceremony.

147
00:09:52,120 --> 00:09:54,840
And a film crew was going to be present.

148
00:09:54,939 --> 00:09:58,720
A film crew from the National Office of the Boy Scouts of America.

149
00:09:58,720 --> 00:10:07,440
And the camp director and the program director who play a key part in the retreat ceremony were going to be absent that day,

150
00:10:07,920 --> 00:10:09,760
owing to a day off they had scheduled.

151
00:10:10,420 --> 00:10:17,060
And so me and my friend George were going to get to stand in the camp director and the program director spots

152
00:10:17,060 --> 00:10:20,420
for that retreat ceremony and be immortalized on Sunday.

153
00:10:20,420 --> 00:10:33,120
So we were excited and nervous about this whole thing.

154
00:10:33,459 --> 00:10:38,660
The film crew had mounted a camera on top of a van.

155
00:10:39,079 --> 00:10:44,439
And there was a road right beside the parade ground so that they could do this kind of sweeping pan.

156
00:10:44,520 --> 00:10:45,579
It was going to be very dramatic.

157
00:10:45,580 --> 00:10:50,460
And they were excited because the retreat ceremony, of course, is very picturesque.

158
00:10:50,680 --> 00:10:55,320
And the retreat ceremony started out and everything was going okay.

159
00:10:55,440 --> 00:11:04,020
And there was a point at which the program director and the camp director spots are supposed to make a couple of turns, you know,

160
00:11:04,400 --> 00:11:07,560
and a couple of salutes and things like that.

161
00:11:08,600 --> 00:11:10,520
And we totally messed it up.

162
00:11:10,520 --> 00:11:14,700
We couldn't remember who was to turn to who and who was to salute to what.

163
00:11:14,700 --> 00:11:18,720
And so we looked a little, we looked a little rag tag here.

164
00:11:26,360 --> 00:11:31,180
And then it came time to strike the colors and fold them up.

165
00:11:31,740 --> 00:11:36,340
And the colors came down the flagpole just fine with the proper bugle call.

166
00:11:36,760 --> 00:11:40,400
And then the fellows started to fold the flag.

167
00:11:40,400 --> 00:11:44,000
And these are senior staff members that are folding the flag, you know.

168
00:11:45,079 --> 00:11:48,480
These are not just like little tenderfoot scouts who are doing this.

169
00:11:49,020 --> 00:11:56,660
The guy who was folding the flag did not notice until too late that he had folded the blue field of stars of the flag inside.

170
00:11:56,660 --> 00:12:01,240
And so the flag fold would not work out at all.

171
00:12:01,760 --> 00:12:05,160
And he got to the point where he finally noticed that.

172
00:12:05,540 --> 00:12:13,060
And he uttered a very unscout-like word under his breath, but loud enough for us to hear, which of course said everybody laughing.

173
00:12:13,060 --> 00:12:18,180
And then he had to unroll the flag altogether and begin folding it again.

174
00:12:18,680 --> 00:12:23,640
So I later saw the tape, and you really couldn't tell that we did anything wrong.

175
00:12:23,800 --> 00:12:30,300
But we were not very happy with ourselves at the end of that particular retreat ceremony.

176
00:12:30,420 --> 00:12:31,560
It was definitely one to remember.

177
00:12:31,560 --> 00:13:01,540
We'll be right back.

178
00:13:01,560 --> 00:13:31,540
We'll be right back.

179
00:13:31,540 --> 00:13:39,020
In our last installment, we talked a little bit about the broad concept of who owns the troop, and we agree that it's the scouts.

180
00:13:39,620 --> 00:13:51,719
And we talked a little bit about some of the reservations that adult leaders usually have over having the youth leadership take over the responsibility for the troop.

181
00:13:52,040 --> 00:13:56,599
First of all, understand that the role of the adults in the troop is going to change drastically.

182
00:13:56,600 --> 00:13:59,300
Your relationship to the scouts is going to change.

183
00:13:59,420 --> 00:14:01,220
What you do at a troop meeting is going to change.

184
00:14:01,380 --> 00:14:04,360
The participation in the life of the troop is going to change.

185
00:14:04,360 --> 00:14:07,440
And what's going to happen is you're going to clam up.

186
00:14:07,700 --> 00:14:08,720
You're going to stop leading.

187
00:14:09,040 --> 00:14:13,560
If you stop leading a little bit, then the scouts have to lead a little bit.

188
00:14:13,920 --> 00:14:17,000
If you stop leading a lot, then the scouts have to lead a lot.

189
00:14:17,100 --> 00:14:19,900
If you stop leading altogether, then the scouts have to lead.

190
00:14:19,900 --> 00:14:20,900
Wow.

191
00:14:21,980 --> 00:14:23,100
Are you really going to do that?

192
00:14:23,640 --> 00:14:28,520
Are you really just going to show up one night at a troop meeting and just shut everything down and say, well, I'm not leading anymore?

193
00:14:29,520 --> 00:14:30,900
It's not going to be that easy.

194
00:14:31,100 --> 00:14:33,840
You're going to have a slight transition period.

195
00:14:33,840 --> 00:14:35,260
But it's going to be slight.

196
00:14:36,080 --> 00:14:43,820
And what you're going to do is you're going to work with and mentor your youth leadership so that they begin to take the reins.

197
00:14:44,500 --> 00:14:51,600
And you need to have a plan and you need to have a set number of meetings that you're going to do until they're ready to take over.

198
00:14:52,160 --> 00:14:55,920
Now, you would think I would advise you to send your youth leaders into a training situation.

199
00:14:56,040 --> 00:15:00,380
And I'm not going to do that because they don't learn to be leaders in a training situation.

200
00:15:00,380 --> 00:15:06,820
They learn to be leaders through a continuous process of coaching and mentoring.

201
00:15:07,280 --> 00:15:09,340
And that's your job as a scout master.

202
00:15:10,080 --> 00:15:16,020
So we're going to begin with some simple things and we're going to let them move on to some bigger things.

203
00:15:16,420 --> 00:15:18,160
They're going to run the opening of the meeting.

204
00:15:18,680 --> 00:15:20,660
They're going to run the patrol part of the meeting.

205
00:15:20,800 --> 00:15:22,220
They're going to run the game part of the meeting.

206
00:15:22,360 --> 00:15:24,720
They're going to run the instructional part of the meeting.

207
00:15:24,940 --> 00:15:26,740
And they're going to run the closing part of the meeting.

208
00:15:26,740 --> 00:15:30,500
And you're going to show up at the closing for about two minutes.

209
00:15:31,360 --> 00:15:33,400
All right, I'm giving you two, not just one.

210
00:15:34,420 --> 00:15:38,400
For about two minutes for a scout master's minute and say something encouraging and wonderful.

211
00:15:38,700 --> 00:15:39,660
And that's going to be it.

212
00:15:40,280 --> 00:15:43,300
The rest of the time, what you're going to be doing is observing.

213
00:15:43,940 --> 00:15:46,740
You're going to be looking and seeing what they're up to.

214
00:15:47,440 --> 00:15:50,060
And you're going to watch them work.

215
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:52,320
You're not going to tell them anything.

216
00:15:52,440 --> 00:15:53,100
You're not going to share.

217
00:15:53,200 --> 00:15:53,880
You're not going to instruct.

218
00:15:53,960 --> 00:15:54,780
You're not going to shout at them.

219
00:15:54,820 --> 00:15:55,620
You're not going to interject.

220
00:15:55,620 --> 00:15:58,100
You're just going to watch them do what they do.

221
00:15:58,880 --> 00:16:03,220
Then you'll have a little time afterwards where you can sit down with your leadership

222
00:16:03,220 --> 00:16:08,080
core, with your senior patrol leader, patrol leaders, you know, your key youth leaders,

223
00:16:08,600 --> 00:16:13,960
where you sit down, not you and six assistant scout masters, because now you have them outnumbered

224
00:16:13,960 --> 00:16:15,260
and they're not going to be able to do anything.

225
00:16:15,900 --> 00:16:20,240
But where you sit down and you just kind of review the meeting very lightly.

226
00:16:20,760 --> 00:16:24,200
You make some suggestions, perhaps, and you ask a lot of questions.

227
00:16:24,200 --> 00:16:25,500
That's the way it's going to work.

228
00:16:26,180 --> 00:16:28,260
The mentoring and coaching process is continuous.

229
00:16:28,600 --> 00:16:31,660
And you and your youth leadership are going to be learning a lot of things together.

230
00:16:32,220 --> 00:16:33,380
You're going to be collaborating.

231
00:16:33,600 --> 00:16:35,960
You're going to be discovering and inventing and trying things out.

232
00:16:36,020 --> 00:16:39,300
And it's really a very exciting and rewarding process.

233
00:16:40,060 --> 00:16:41,660
And now think about coaching.

234
00:16:42,300 --> 00:16:46,320
Coaching takes place mainly away from the playing field, correct?

235
00:16:46,320 --> 00:16:50,800
So when it's troop meeting time, that's not your coaching time.

236
00:16:51,400 --> 00:16:54,240
Your coaching time is mainly away from the playing field.

237
00:16:54,580 --> 00:16:56,540
And we're going to be talking about where to fit that in.

238
00:16:56,900 --> 00:16:57,940
What is this going to look like?

239
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:00,000
Well, I'll tell you exactly what it's going to look like.

240
00:17:00,100 --> 00:17:01,400
You've been to a Pinewood Derby.

241
00:17:02,200 --> 00:17:06,140
And I think you'll agree with me that there's three basic kinds of Pinewood Derby cars.

242
00:17:06,140 --> 00:17:07,600
All right?

243
00:17:07,740 --> 00:17:13,180
The first one is the one that the Cub Scout had absolutely no help with at all.

244
00:17:13,880 --> 00:17:21,140
He took the kit out of the box, put it together in about five minutes, slapped a little paint on it, and covered it with decals.

245
00:17:21,480 --> 00:17:27,800
And he's as proud of that thing as he is of any Maserati that's on the street out there.

246
00:17:27,800 --> 00:17:37,600
The second kind shows a lot of his own work, but it also has some refinements that indicate that he had some help.

247
00:17:38,180 --> 00:17:46,980
And the third kind of Pinewood Derby car is the one that shows up to the Pinewood Derby in a shoebox, you know, packed in cotton,

248
00:17:47,440 --> 00:17:52,659
because nobody is allowed to touch it because somebody else basically made it for him while he watched.

249
00:17:52,660 --> 00:17:57,940
Your troop is going to be a combination of the first two types of Pinewood Derby car.

250
00:17:58,900 --> 00:18:07,600
It's going to be Scout Made and Scout Run, but it will show a couple of little refinements here and there that indicate that somebody's watching this store.

251
00:18:08,420 --> 00:18:09,800
It might not be the fastest.

252
00:18:09,980 --> 00:18:11,680
It might not be the slickest looking one.

253
00:18:11,840 --> 00:18:15,380
It might not win any beauty prizes, but it's going to function just fine.

254
00:18:15,380 --> 00:18:22,900
And it's going to inspire a lot of admiration for the people who create it, which is the Scouts who own the troop, right?

255
00:18:24,180 --> 00:18:30,880
Prepare for a blow to the old ego, because if you're a very involved Scoutmaster and you're used to running a lot of things,

256
00:18:30,960 --> 00:18:36,360
making a lot of decisions, making all the plans, and, you know, presenting the program for the boys,

257
00:18:36,420 --> 00:18:37,980
this is going to hurt the old ego a bit.

258
00:18:38,100 --> 00:18:40,740
And I am speaking from experience.

259
00:18:40,740 --> 00:18:44,800
So I'm not speaking from an ivory tower somewhere.

260
00:18:45,680 --> 00:18:49,300
I'm not speaking as though this isn't something that I have to deal with all the time.

261
00:18:49,380 --> 00:18:53,560
But here's a couple of useful things that have really helped me along the way.

262
00:18:53,980 --> 00:18:58,680
And one of them is that I have learned that this has absolutely nothing to do with me.

263
00:18:59,040 --> 00:19:01,280
It's not a reflection on me as a person.

264
00:19:02,180 --> 00:19:03,080
What do I mean by that?

265
00:19:03,140 --> 00:19:07,020
Well, your Scouts are going to make some bad decisions.

266
00:19:07,020 --> 00:19:12,180
They're going to act like their age, which can be really difficult sometimes.

267
00:19:12,540 --> 00:19:15,120
And they're going to be fairly inconsiderate at times.

268
00:19:16,100 --> 00:19:19,580
And, you know, the inverse is also going to be true.

269
00:19:19,639 --> 00:19:20,800
They're going to make wonderful decisions.

270
00:19:20,960 --> 00:19:22,580
And they're going to act well beyond their age.

271
00:19:22,879 --> 00:19:25,940
And they're going to be very considerate and compassionate at times.

272
00:19:26,820 --> 00:19:31,300
But no matter what, you know, if they're being good, if they're being bad,

273
00:19:31,360 --> 00:19:33,420
it doesn't really have anything to do with you.

274
00:19:33,560 --> 00:19:35,139
It's not a personal statement about you.

275
00:19:35,140 --> 00:19:41,060
It's not a personal statement about your ability to lead, inspire, coach, or mentor them.

276
00:19:41,260 --> 00:19:45,280
What it is, is it is them being themselves.

277
00:19:45,920 --> 00:19:50,700
And whatever we're going to have to deal with to get over that, you're going to deal with it.

278
00:19:50,960 --> 00:19:55,540
Because them being themselves is so much more interesting, so much better for them,

279
00:19:56,020 --> 00:19:58,220
and so much better for all the Scouts in the troop,

280
00:19:58,620 --> 00:20:02,860
that it is well worth any small price that we might have to pay to our egos.

281
00:20:02,860 --> 00:20:08,620
So we'll talk about this and wrap this up in our third installment on the next podcast.

282
00:20:08,620 --> 00:20:36,080
That's right.

283
00:20:36,080 --> 00:20:39,300
It's time for a Scoutmaster's Minute.

284
00:20:39,899 --> 00:20:42,439
I'm on my hands.

285
00:20:42,540 --> 00:20:43,840
Do, do, do.

286
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:44,699
Me, me, pie.

287
00:20:44,899 --> 00:20:45,659
Me, me, pie.

288
00:20:45,860 --> 00:20:46,620
Me, me, pie.

289
00:20:46,820 --> 00:20:47,600
Me, me, pie.

290
00:20:47,780 --> 00:20:48,220
Me, me, pie.

291
00:20:48,360 --> 00:20:50,760
Let me ask you a question.

292
00:20:50,860 --> 00:20:53,699
Are you worried about where your next meal is coming from?

293
00:20:53,780 --> 00:20:58,960
Are you worried about having a safe place out of the weather to lay down and sleep tonight?

294
00:20:58,960 --> 00:21:05,920
Are you worried about the immediate safety of your family and the immediate health of your community

295
00:21:05,920 --> 00:21:08,600
and the stability of your family and your children?

296
00:21:08,840 --> 00:21:09,460
Probably not.

297
00:21:09,540 --> 00:21:12,540
If you live in the United States, you probably have it pretty good.

298
00:21:12,660 --> 00:21:18,760
No, there's a lot of need and trouble out there and things that we need to help with in our own country,

299
00:21:18,880 --> 00:21:20,580
but we have it pretty good, don't we?

300
00:21:20,580 --> 00:21:26,399
And I'm thinking of the people of Haiti and I'm thinking of the people of Chile

301
00:21:26,399 --> 00:21:34,360
who are undergoing this chaotic, cataclysmic natural events that have happened in the past several weeks and months,

302
00:21:34,560 --> 00:21:36,580
and they really need our help.

303
00:21:36,879 --> 00:21:38,179
And what's the best way to do that?

304
00:21:38,220 --> 00:21:42,740
Well, we can't all suit up and go to Haiti, and we can't fly down to Chile and start helping out.

305
00:21:42,740 --> 00:21:49,620
I was inspired to read about the scouts in Chile who are helping clear rubble and make up emergency packages

306
00:21:49,620 --> 00:21:54,360
and who are generally assisting with aid to their own country.

307
00:21:54,980 --> 00:21:58,080
Well, there's a way for us to help, and it comes down to this.

308
00:21:58,140 --> 00:22:03,440
It comes down to a very few dollars, and I think we can all afford that very few dollars.

309
00:22:04,160 --> 00:22:09,540
UNICEF is a United Nations arm that is concerned with the health and welfare of children,

310
00:22:09,540 --> 00:22:16,760
and the American Red Cross is well known as lending disaster relief in these situations,

311
00:22:17,139 --> 00:22:20,440
and they will benefit from us sending money.

312
00:22:20,860 --> 00:22:25,000
If we all sent a little bit, just imagine the immense impact it would have.

313
00:22:25,440 --> 00:22:30,340
If the scout law means anything, it means that we're going to help when help is needed.

314
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:35,940
If this amazing technological marvel called the World Wide Web means anything,

315
00:22:35,940 --> 00:22:41,620
it means that we're going to understand that people need help, and we're going to be able to send it.

316
00:22:41,960 --> 00:22:46,060
We have so much, and it takes so little to help out.

317
00:22:46,240 --> 00:22:51,960
I'm going to have a link to the UNICEF and the American Red Cross efforts in Chile,

318
00:22:52,160 --> 00:22:54,360
and I hope that you will follow that.

319
00:22:54,480 --> 00:22:57,500
I'll have it at scoutmaster.typepad.com.

320
00:22:57,920 --> 00:22:58,680
Follow that up.

321
00:22:59,140 --> 00:23:00,020
Send a few dollars.

322
00:23:00,020 --> 00:23:03,760
And consider making a monthly pledge to either one of those organizations,

323
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:07,820
because they're worthy and they eliminate a lot of suffering in the world.

324
00:23:07,980 --> 00:23:13,180
So we have a lot, and we pledge to help, and here's an excellent opportunity for us to do so.

325
00:23:13,180 --> 00:23:30,700
Hey, thanks again for listening.

326
00:23:31,080 --> 00:23:36,280
You can read the Scoutmaster blog at scoutmaster.typepad.com,

327
00:23:36,280 --> 00:23:42,580
and follow us at Scoutmaster Blog on Facebook and Scoutmaster Blog on Twitter.

328
00:23:43,480 --> 00:23:46,600
You can subscribe to the Scoutmaster Podcast on iTunes,

329
00:23:46,980 --> 00:23:50,520
and you can feel free just to leave a comment, a review, or a rating,

330
00:23:50,620 --> 00:23:53,300
and I really appreciate those of you who have,

331
00:23:53,379 --> 00:23:56,860
and the emails and things that we've received over time.

332
00:23:57,240 --> 00:24:05,000
You can email me, Clark Green, with your comments and questions at clarkgreen.gmail.com.

333
00:24:05,000 --> 00:24:07,340
Clark is C-L-A-R-K-E.

334
00:24:07,600 --> 00:24:10,160
Green is G-R-E-E-N, all one word.

335
00:24:11,140 --> 00:24:16,220
The Scoutmaster Blog and the Scoutmaster Podcast are not official publications of the Boy Scouts of America,

336
00:24:16,320 --> 00:24:19,440
nor are they endorsed or sponsored by the Boy Scouts of America.

337
00:24:20,260 --> 00:24:23,480
No, they're just really intended to lend a hand to scout leaders

338
00:24:23,480 --> 00:24:25,600
and perhaps have a bit of fun along the way,

339
00:24:25,700 --> 00:24:28,140
so I certainly appreciate you listening.

340
00:24:28,860 --> 00:24:31,420
Sir Robert, do you have anything to tell the folks?

341
00:24:31,700 --> 00:24:33,940
Good luck to you, and good camping.

342
00:24:33,940 --> 00:24:34,460
Thanks.

343
00:24:35,000 --> 00:24:35,840
See you again soon.

344
00:24:35,840 --> 00:24:35,860
See you again soon.

